/**
 * A short introduction to each chapter, for its page under /cbse/ (worker/seo.ts).
 * Keyed by chapter key (subject|class|title). Plain text.
 */
export const CHAPTER_INTROS: Record<string, string> = {
  // ---------- Class 9 Science ----------
  "biology|9|Cell: The Building Block of Life":
    "Every living thing is made of cells, and this chapter looks inside them. You will learn how microscopes made cells visible, what cell theory says, and how prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells differ. It covers the cell membrane and cell wall, diffusion and osmosis, and the work of the nucleus, mitochondria, plastids, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes and vacuoles. It ends with chromosomes, DNA and the two kinds of cell division, mitosis and meiosis. Questions often ask you to match an organelle with its job or to compare plant and animal cells.",
  "biology|9|Tissues in Action":
    "A tissue is a group of cells that work together on one job. This chapter starts with plants: the meristems that make new cells, and the simple and complex permanent tissues that store food, give support and carry water. It then turns to animals and their epithelial, connective, muscular and nervous tissues, including blood, tendons and ligaments, and shows how bones, joints and muscles move the body together. Learn where each tissue is found and how its structure suits its work, because most questions test that link.",
  "biology|9|Reproduction: How Life Continues":
    "Living things make more of their own kind in two ways, asexual and sexual. This chapter covers budding, spore formation and vegetative propagation, along with grafting, layering and tissue culture used by growers. It then explains the parts of a flower, pollination, fertilisation, and how fruits and seeds form and spread. The last part looks at animals: external and internal fertilisation, metamorphosis, and the human reproductive systems. Pay attention to why meiosis and sexual reproduction bring variation while mitosis gives clones.",
  "biology|9|Patterns in Life: Diversity and Classification":
    "There are so many kinds of living things that scientists need an orderly way to group them. This chapter explains why we classify, how the hierarchy of groups works, and how binomial nomenclature gives each species its scientific name. It goes through the five kingdoms, the main divisions of plants, and animals from sponges to vertebrates. It also covers viruses, lichens, fossils as evidence, endemic species and biodiversity hotspots. A good way to revise is to list one clear feature and one example for every group.",
  "chemistry|9|Exploring Mixtures and their Separation":
    "Most things around us are mixtures. This chapter shows how to tell homogeneous from heterogeneous mixtures, how the Tyndall effect helps, and how to write concentration as mass by mass, mass by volume and volume by volume percentage. You will read solubility curves and use them to understand crystallisation. The second half is about separation: evaporation, distillation, fractional distillation, chromatography, sublimation and the separating funnel. Practise the percentage sums, and be ready to choose the right method for a given mixture and give the reason.",
  "chemistry|9|Journey Inside the Atom":
    "This chapter follows how our idea of the atom changed as new evidence came in. It begins with early atomic ideas and the discovery of the electron, then moves through Thomson's model, Rutherford's gold foil experiment and Bohr's model, with the limits of each. You will learn about protons, neutrons and electrons, atomic number and mass number, how electrons fill shells, and valency. It ends with isotopes, isobars, average atomic mass and the uses of isotopes. Electronic configurations and isotope sums come up often, so practise both.",
  "chemistry|9|Atomic Foundations of Matter":
    "How do atoms join to make everything we see? This chapter begins with two laws, conservation of mass and constant proportions, and shows how Dalton's atomic theory explains them. It then covers molecules of elements and compounds, covalent bonds formed by sharing electrons, and ionic bonds formed by transferring them. You will learn to name compounds, write their formulas, and work out molecular mass and formula unit mass. Many questions are short calculations, so keep the masses of common elements at your fingertips.",
  "physics|9|Describing Motion Around Us":
    "This chapter gives you the language of motion. It starts with rest, motion and position, then separates distance from displacement and speed from velocity. You will read position-time and velocity-time graphs, find slopes and areas, and work out average acceleration. The kinematic equations follow, with everyday uses such as stopping distance and road safety. The chapter closes with uniform circular motion, where speed stays the same but velocity keeps changing direction. Graph reading and kinematic sums are the main skills to practise here.",
  "physics|9|How Forces Affect Motion":
    "Forces can start, stop, speed up, slow down or turn an object. This chapter explains balanced and unbalanced forces, friction, and Newton's three laws of motion. You will meet inertia, momentum and the second law that links force, mass and acceleration, and see the third law at work in recoiling guns and rockets. It also treats two or more objects as one system. Draw the forces on each object before you calculate, and remember that an action and its reaction act on two different bodies.",
  "physics|9|Work, Energy, and Simple Machines":
    "In science, work is done only when a force moves something along its direction. This chapter defines work, including positive, negative and zero work, and connects it to kinetic energy through the work-energy theorem. You will study potential energy, the conservation of mechanical energy, changes from one form of energy to another, and power. Simple machines come next: levers of all three classes, pulleys and inclined planes. Learn the formulas with their units, and remember that a machine makes work easier but does not reduce the work itself.",
  "physics|9|Sound Waves: Characteristics and Applications":
    "Sound begins with a vibration and needs a medium to travel. This chapter explains how compressions and rarefactions carry sound, and why there is silence in a vacuum. You will learn wavelength, frequency, time period and amplitude, and how they decide pitch and loudness. It compares the speed of sound in solids, liquids and gases, looks at the effect of temperature, and explains how the ear hears. Practise the relation between speed, frequency and wavelength, and reading values from a wave graph.",
  "physics|9|Earth as a System: Energy, Matter, and Life":
    "The Earth works as one system in which land, water, air and living things affect each other. This chapter follows the Sun's energy: electromagnetic waves, insolation, albedo, re-radiation and the greenhouse effect. It describes the layers and composition of the atmosphere, the ozone layer, winds and ocean currents. It then covers the water cycle and other biogeochemical cycles, and how human activity is changing the climate. Focus on cause and effect, such as why cities become heat islands or how a melting ice cover changes albedo.",

  // ---------- Class 9 Maths ----------
  "maths|9|Orienting Yourself: The Use of Coordinates":
    "Coordinates let you say exactly where something is. Starting from the floor plan of a room, this chapter builds up the two axes, the four quadrants and the correct way to write a point as an ordered pair. You will plot points, spot lines parallel to the axes, and reflect points in an axis. The later part uses right triangles to find the distance between two points and from the origin. Watch the order of the two numbers and the signs in each quadrant, as these cause most mistakes.",
  "maths|9|Introduction to Linear Polynomials":
    "This chapter introduces polynomials and then looks closely at the simplest useful kind, the linear polynomial. You will name terms, coefficients, constants and degree, and find the value of an expression. Linear growth and decay in daily life lead to the form y = ax + b, where a is the slope and b is the y-intercept. You will draw lines, see where they meet the axes, and recognise parallel lines. Practise moving between a story, a table, an equation and a graph, since questions use all four.",
  "maths|9|The World of Numbers":
    "Numbers have a long story, from tally marks on bones to zero and negative numbers. This chapter follows that story and then works with integers, rational numbers and real numbers. You will use Brahmagupta's rules, the properties of rational numbers, and the idea that between any two rationals there is always another. Irrational numbers come next, with a proof by contradiction and a way to mark root 2 on the number line. Practise operations with fractions and comparing real numbers until they feel easy.",
  "maths|9|Exploring Algebraic Identities":
    "An identity is true for every value of its variables. This chapter shows why the standard identities work, using area models, algebra tiles and cubes cut into pieces. You will expand squares and cubes, square a trinomial, factorise by splitting the middle term, and simplify rational expressions. It also uses identities for quick mental maths and for neat proofs, such as why n³ − n is always divisible by 6. Learn each identity as a picture as well as a formula, and you will rarely forget a term.",
  "maths|9|I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round":
    "This chapter is about circles. It starts with the definition of a circle, its arcs, chords and diameter, and its symmetries. You will find how many circles pass through two or three points, and construct the circumcircle of a triangle. The main theorems link chords with the angles they make at the centre, with perpendiculars from the centre, and with distances from it, and then move to angles subtended by arcs. Draw a clear figure for every question. Most length problems come down to a right triangle with the radius as hypotenuse.",
  "maths|9|Measuring Space: Perimeter and Area":
    "Why do runners in outer lanes start further ahead? This chapter answers that and more. It begins with perimeter, the circumference of a circle and the number π, then finds arc lengths and the perimeter of sectors and composite shapes. Next come the areas of parallelograms and triangles, and Heron's formula for a triangle when only its three sides are known. Keep arc length and area apart, check units, and practise breaking a tricky outline into simple parts before you calculate.",
  "maths|9|The Mathematics of Maybe: Introduction to Probability":
    "Probability puts a number on how likely something is, from 0 for impossible to 1 for certain. This chapter compares subjective, experimental and theoretical probability, and explains how results settle down over many trials. You will list sample spaces, use tree diagrams, work with coins, dice and spinners, and find probability from areas. It also explains the gambler's fallacy, the false belief that past results change a fair coin. List every equally likely outcome first; the fraction usually follows easily.",
  "maths|9|Predicting What Comes Next: Exploring Sequences and Progressions":
    "A sequence follows a rule, and once you know it you can predict any term. This chapter covers triangular and odd numbers, the Virahanka-Fibonacci sequence, and the difference between explicit and recursive rules. It then studies arithmetic progressions, with the nth term, sums and graphs, and geometric progressions, where each term is multiplied by the same number. Practise finding the first term and common difference from two given terms, and deciding whether a pattern is arithmetic, geometric or neither.",
  "maths|9|Propositions and Their Converses":
    "Mathematics depends on careful reasoning, and this chapter teaches how it works. A proposition can be true while its converse is false, so each needs its own check. You will write converses, look for counterexamples, and follow proofs, including the converse of the Pythagoras theorem. Examples range from rain and wet roads to multiples, factor pairs and areas. When a question says 'if P then Q', write the converse in words first, test it with a few cases, and only then try to prove it.",
  "maths|9|How Quantities Combine: Understanding Data":
    "The average of two averages is often not the true average. This chapter explains why through the weighted mean. You will combine groups of different sizes, mix liquids and metals of different purity, and work with ratings that carry different weights. The balance-point picture helps you see where a weighted mean must lie. It also covers reading cluster-column and stacked bar charts. Remember that the larger group pulls the mean towards itself, and use that to check whether your answer is reasonable.",
  "maths|9|The World of Algorithms":
    "An algorithm is a clear set of steps that always leads to the answer. This chapter looks at familiar ones, such as column addition, and asks why each step is needed. It then builds algorithms to list divisors, find common divisors and work out the greatest common divisor, and shows how to make them faster. You will read flowcharts with decisions and loops and trace them line by line. Keep a small table of values as you trace; it is the surest way to avoid errors.",
  "maths|9|Quadrilaterals":
    "Four-sided figures look simple but are full of patterns. This chapter defines convex and non-convex quadrilaterals, then proves the properties of parallelograms: their sides, angles and diagonals, and the tests that show a figure is one. Rhombus, rectangle, square, kite and trapezium follow, with the midsegment of a trapezium and several area results. Learn the difference between a property and a test, since questions ask for both. A table of shapes against their diagonal properties is a very useful revision tool.",
  "maths|9|Two Variables, One Line":
    "An equation such as ax + by + c = 0 has many solutions, and together they form a straight line. This chapter covers forming linear equations, checking solutions, finding intercepts and understanding slope as rise over run. You will compare lines that are parallel, horizontal, vertical or pass through the origin. The last part solves pairs of equations by substitution and elimination, and checks when a pair has one solution, none or many. Practise turning word problems into equations, which is where most marks are lost.",
  "maths|9|Math of Space: Surface Area and Volume":
    "This chapter measures solids. It starts with cubes and cuboids, including packing problems and painted cubes that are cut into smaller ones. Cylinders and cones come next, with curved and total surface area, slant height and volume, along with pyramids and nets. Many questions are about melting and recasting, where the volume stays the same but the surface area changes. Before you calculate, decide whether the question needs surface area or volume, and note which faces are open.",

  // ---------- Class 9 English ----------
  "english|9|How I Taught My Grandmother to Read":
    "A young girl reads a serialised novel to her grandmother, who cannot read. When the girl is away, the grandmother feels so helpless that she decides to learn the alphabet herself. Sudha Murty's story is about dignity, determination and the joy of learning at any age. Practice here covers characters, theme, cause and effect, inference and extracts, along with idioms, prefixes, the past perfect tense and letter writing. Poem questions look at imagery, symbolism and structure. Read the story once for feeling and once for detail.",
  "english|9|The Pot Maker":
    "Sentila longs to make pots, but her mother, Arenla, knows how hard the work is and wishes something else for her. The story follows Sentila's wish, her training and the views of the village elders, who believe a skill belongs to the community. Practice questions cover the pot-making process, the order of events, Sentila's feelings and the meaning of the ending. You will also work on glossary words, noun clauses, determiners and similes, and on a poem about vocations and its metaphors.",
  "english|9|Winds of Change":
    "The humble hand fan, or pankha, tells a story about India. This essay traces its early evidence and shows how fans differ from place to place, from Rajasthan's appliqué work to West Bengal's sola fans and tribal designs. It also asks what happens to a craft when life changes. Practice covers how the essay is organised, fact and opinion, regional identity and vocabulary, with grammar on the present perfect tense, collocations and fixed expressions. Writing tasks focus on describing an artefact clearly without adding opinions.",
  "english|9|Vitamin-M":
    "Ravi secretly follows his grandfather on a day out and finds that the old man is far sharper than the family believes. The story uses gentle humour and irony to make us think about memory, independence and how we treat older people. Practice covers the plot, the title, Grandpa's awareness, Ravi's dilemma and who said what. There are also questions on 'The Lost Child' and on poems about remembering, with their imagery, refrain and poetic devices, as well as vocabulary and article writing.",
  "english|9|The World of Limitless Possibilities":
    "This text celebrates the Paralympics and India's place in them, with a focus on Dr Deepa Malik, her challenges, her breakthrough and her work with young people. It asks readers to look at ability and determination with fresh eyes. Practice covers facts and opinions, tone, cause and effect, the title and the speaker's message. Grammar work includes modal auxiliaries and reported speech, and the writing section teaches notice format. Learn the tense and pronoun changes in reported speech carefully, as they appear in many questions.",
  "english|9|Twin Melodies":
    "This play brings together tradition and modern music through Shruti and Nabin. Over three acts, hurt feelings and strict rules give way to understanding, and each character learns to respect another's style. Practice covers the setting, what happens in each act, who said a line and why, and how characters change by the final scene. You will also study dramatic devices such as the aside, the use of the ellipsis in a script, figurative phrases and idioms. Reading the dialogue aloud helps a great deal.",
  "english|9|Carrier of Words":
    "Khetaram is a rural postman who carries letters, money and news across the desert to scattered dhaanis. The text shows his long working day, the heat, the trust people place in him, and the letter he dreads to deliver. It also explains how Gramin Dak Sewaks fit into the postal system. Practice covers details, extracts, inference and instances of trust, with vocabulary, metaphor, alliteration and uses of the present perfect tense. Notice how small details reveal his character without the writer stating it.",
  "english|9|Follow That Dream":
    "Through a letter about Ming's dream, this chapter asks what it takes to turn a wish into a plan. It looks at cost, sacrifice, support and the reasons many dreams remain only dreams. Practice covers inference, analogy, word meanings and critical reflection on the advice given. Grammar work includes the first conditional, the uses of 'could' and polite requests, while the writing part teaches the format and formal tone of an email. Poem questions look at comfort, change and the status quo.",

  // ---------- Class 9 Social Science ----------
  "history|9|Early Humans and Beginning of Civilisation":
    "This chapter goes back to a time before writing. It explains how tools, bones and layers of soil tell us about early people, and how humans evolved and spread out of Africa. You will compare the Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Neolithic periods, study rock art, and see how farming changed life at places such as Mehrgarh. It then turns to Harappan cities, their weights and water systems, and to Mesopotamia and other river-valley civilisations. A simple timeline is the best way to keep periods and sites in order.",
  "history|9|State and Society up to 1000 CE":
    "How did small clans grow into kingdoms and empires? This chapter follows political life from the Vedic janas and assemblies to janapadas, mahājanapadas and the rise of Magadha, and on to the kingdoms of Tamilakam. It explains the saptāṁga idea of the state, the duties of a king, councils of ministers and the way empires were run. Inscriptions and land grants are used as evidence, including the Uttaramerur inscription on Chola village assemblies. Practise reading BCE and CE timelines and linking each source with what it proves.",
  "history|9|Resistance and Resilience (1000–1700 CE)":
    "Between 1000 and 1700 CE many Indian kingdoms and communities faced invasion and fought back in different ways. This chapter covers resistance to the Delhi Sultanate, Mewar and its guerrilla warfare, Rajput and Mughal relations, the Ahoms and their navy, the Vijayanagara Empire, the Marathas and their forts, and the Sikhs and Jats. It asks what resistance and resilience mean beyond the battlefield. Keep a table of leaders, regions and methods, since questions often ask you to match them or to put events in order.",
  "geography|9|Shaping of the Earth’s Surface":
    "The land around us is always being built up and worn down. This chapter begins inside the Earth, with its layers, convection currents and tectonic plates, and explains earthquakes and volcanoes. It then looks at the surface, where weathering, erosion and deposition by rivers, waves, glaciers, wind and underground water make landforms such as meanders, deltas, beaches and dunes. It also covers earthquake safety and soil and water conservation. Learn each landform with the agent that makes it, and whether it is formed by erosion or deposition.",
  "geography|9|Atmosphere and Climate":
    "A thin blanket of air makes life possible. This chapter covers the composition and layers of the atmosphere, how temperature changes with height, and the parts played by pressure, wind, humidity and precipitation. It explains the difference between weather and climate, land and sea breezes, and the seasons of India. The monsoon gets special attention: how it works, how people have predicted it, and why it matters so much. Focus on what makes air move, because pressure differences explain most of the chapter.",
  "geography|9|Oceans and Life":
    "Oceans cover most of our planet and shape life on land as well. This chapter describes ocean relief, the shape of the sea floor, and the movements of water: waves, tides and currents. It explains cyclones and tsunamis, then looks at marine resources and the ways people depend on the sea. It also introduces maritime zones and the rules that decide who may use which part of the ocean. Be clear about the cause of each movement, as waves, tides and currents are often confused.",
  "geography|9|Life on Earth":
    "Why do rainforests, deserts and tundra lie where they do? This chapter introduces biomes and the temperature and rainfall that shape them. It visits rainforests, taiga and temperate forests, grasslands, deserts, tundra and aquatic biomes, and shows how plants and animals are adapted to each. It then covers biodiversity, hotspots, conservation and wildlife corridors, along with the ways people and nature affect each other. Link every biome to its climate first; the plants and animals are then much easier to remember.",
  "politics|9|Democracy":
    "Democracy is more than voting. This chapter explains its meaning, its early traditions in India, the freedom struggle and the work of the Constituent Assembly. It covers popular sovereignty, universal adult franchise, representation, separation of powers, rule of law and Fundamental Rights, and shows how checks and balances keep power accountable. You will also look at the media, the multi-party system, the protection of vulnerable groups and democracies in other countries. For each feature, be ready with one Indian example and a reason why it matters.",
  "politics|9|Elections":
    "Elections turn the will of the people into a government. This chapter compares direct and indirect elections and explains how First-Past-The-Post, proportional representation and the single transferable vote work, with simple calculations of winners, margins, seats and quotas. It then follows the process in India: delimitation, electoral rolls, the Election Commission, the schedule, campaign rules and electoral offences. Practise the number questions, and learn the stages of an election in order, because sequence questions are common here.",
  "politics|9|Governance and Public Policy":
    "Choosing a government is only the beginning; this chapter asks how a country is run well. It explains governance, how it differs from government, and India's older ideas of duty and good rule. You will study the features of good governance, the challenges it faces, and e-governance with its benefits and limits. The second half is about public policy: what makes a policy public, who takes part, and the stages of the policy cycle. Case studies are important, so practise naming the stage each one shows.",
  "economics|9|Understanding Social Science":
    "This opening chapter explains what Social Science is and how it differs from the natural sciences. It introduces the four core disciplines, Geography, History, Political Science and Economics, and shows how one problem, such as a drought, can be studied from each side. You will learn how social scientists use evidence, the kinds of historical sources, and ideas from Indian knowledge traditions such as Vasudhaiva kuṭumbakam and the Arthaśhāstra. Know which discipline asks which kind of question, as many items test exactly that.",
  "economics|9|Building Blocks in Economics: The Problem of Choice":
    "We cannot have everything, so we choose. This chapter explains needs and wants, scarcity and the three key questions of what, how and for whom to produce. It covers the factors of production, opportunity cost and the production possibility curve, with simple number work. You will also learn how planned, market and mixed economies answer those questions, what the Economic Survey is, and how India's system has changed. Practise finding opportunity cost from a table, since it is the idea the whole chapter is built on.",
  "economics|9|The Price Puzzle: What Drives the Market":
    "Why are mangoes costly one month and cheap the next? This chapter explains demand, supply and the price at which they meet. You will draw demand and supply curves, add up market demand, and study substitutes, complements, income, seasons and other causes of change. It shows how shortages and surpluses move prices, and why governments sometimes set price ceilings and floors or provide public goods. Practise reading schedules and graphs, and always say whether a change moves along a curve or shifts it.",
  "economics|9|Managing Your Personal Finances":
    "Handling money well is a skill anyone can learn. This chapter covers sources of income, making a budget, saving first and spending later, and why inflation reduces what money can buy. It explains compounding and the advantage of starting early, then compares fixed deposits, bonds, shares and mutual funds by risk and return. Insurance, borrowing, EMIs and income tax complete the picture. Expect short calculations on percentages, compound interest and tax slabs, so practise writing each step clearly.",
  "economics|9|From Ideas to Startups":
    "A business starts with an idea that solves a problem. This chapter explains entrepreneurship through a school stall, a Jataka tale, Lijjat Papad, the Oberoi hotels and other Indian examples. You will learn what goes into a business plan, the difference between fixed and variable costs, how to work out profit, and the four functional areas of a business. It also covers startups, barriers to growth and social responsibility. Practise profit and cost sums, and link each task in a business with its functional area.",

  // ---------- Class 10 Science ----------
  "biology|10|Life Processes":
    "Life processes are the jobs that keep an organism alive: nutrition, respiration, transport and excretion. This chapter explains why large organisms cannot depend on diffusion, how plants make food through photosynthesis, and how stomata control the exchange of gases. It follows food through the human alimentary canal, compares aerobic and anaerobic respiration, and covers the activities on starch, carbon dioxide and saliva. Diagrams carry many marks here, so practise labelling them. Learn each enzyme with where it acts and what it breaks down.",
  "biology|10|Control and Coordination":
    "This chapter explains how living things sense changes and respond to them. In animals, the nervous system uses neurons, synapses and reflex arcs, with the brain and spinal cord in charge, while hormones from the endocrine glands act more slowly. In plants, movements such as phototropism depend on growth and on plant hormones. You will study reflex action, parts of the brain, hormone disorders and feedback. Make a table of glands, hormones and functions, and be ready to compare nervous and hormonal control.",
  "biology|10|How do Organisms Reproduce?":
    "Reproduction keeps a species going and brings the small variations that help it survive. This chapter covers the copying of DNA, and asexual methods such as fission, fragmentation, regeneration, budding, vegetative propagation and spore formation. It then explains sexual reproduction in flowering plants, from pollination to germination, and in human beings, including puberty, the reproductive systems, menstruation, the placenta and reproductive health. Practise labelled diagrams of the flower and the human reproductive systems, which are asked very often.",
  "biology|10|Heredity":
    "Why do children resemble their parents and yet differ from them? This chapter explains how variation builds up and how traits are inherited. It follows Mendel's experiments with pea plants, dominant and recessive traits, and the independent inheritance of two traits. You will learn how genes on chromosomes control characters, how gametes carry them, and how sex is determined in human beings. Practise crosses for one and two traits until you can predict the ratios with confidence, and be clear about which parent decides the sex of the child.",
  "biology|10|Our Environment":
    "An ecosystem is made up of living things and the surroundings they depend on. This chapter covers producers, consumers and decomposers, food chains and webs, trophic levels and the flow of energy, including why food chains are short. It explains biological magnification, the damage to the ozone layer by CFCs, and the difference between biodegradable and non-biodegradable waste. It is a short chapter that gives easy marks. Practise energy-flow sums and be clear about which organism collects the most harmful chemicals.",
  "chemistry|10|Chemical Reactions and Equations":
    "This chapter teaches you to recognise a chemical change and to write it as a balanced equation with physical states. It covers combination, decomposition, displacement and double displacement reactions, along with exothermic and endothermic changes. Oxidation and reduction are explained with everyday examples, including the corrosion of metals and rancidity of food. Balancing equations is the basic skill, so practise it daily. For each type of reaction, learn one equation and what you would observe, such as a colour change or a gas.",
  "chemistry|10|Acids, Bases and Salts":
    "From lemon juice to soap, acids and bases are everywhere. This chapter covers indicators, the reactions of acids with metals, carbonates and bases, and neutralisation. It explains the part played by ions in water, the pH scale, and the importance of pH in daily life. The last part is about salts and chemicals made from common salt, including bleaching powder, baking soda and washing soda, and water of crystallisation. Learn the equations with their products, and remember that acid must be added to water, never the reverse.",
  "chemistry|10|Metals and Non-metals":
    "This chapter compares metals and non-metals by their physical and chemical properties, with the exceptions that examiners like to ask about. You will study reactions with oxygen, water and acids, the reactivity series and displacement. It explains how metals and non-metals form ionic compounds and why these have high melting points. The second half covers the extraction and refining of metals, corrosion and its prevention, and alloys. Learn the reactivity series in order, because it decides both the reactions and the method of extraction.",
  "chemistry|10|Carbon and its Compounds":
    "Carbon forms more compounds than almost any other element because its atoms share electrons and join into chains, branches and rings. This chapter covers covalent bonding, allotropes, saturated and unsaturated compounds, homologous series, functional groups and naming. It then looks at combustion, oxidation, addition and substitution, at ethanol and ethanoic acid, and at how soaps and detergents clean. Practise drawing electron dot structures and naming compounds from their structures. Reactions of ethanol and ethanoic acid are favourite questions.",
  "physics|10|Light – Reflection and Refraction":
    "Light travels in straight lines until something sends it back or bends it. This chapter covers reflection from plane and spherical mirrors, the images formed by concave and convex mirrors, and their uses. It then explains refraction through a glass slab and lenses, refractive index, Snell's law and the power of a lens. The sign convention matters in every numerical, so fix it first. Practise ray diagrams for each position of the object, along with the mirror, lens and magnification formulas.",
  "physics|10|The Human Eye and the Colourful World":
    "This chapter begins with the eye: its parts, how it forms an image, and how it adjusts to see near and far objects. It explains myopia, hypermetropia and presbyopia, and the lenses that correct them. It then turns to the colours around us, with refraction through a prism, dispersion, rainbows, twinkling stars and the scattering of light. Practise ray diagrams for defects of vision and their correction, and be ready to explain in a few clear steps why the sky is blue and the setting Sun is red.",
  "physics|10|Electricity":
    "This chapter explains electric current, charge and potential difference, and how Ohm's law connects current with voltage. You will study resistance, the factors that affect it, resistivity, and resistors joined in series and in parallel. The heating effect of current follows, with its uses in bulbs and fuses, and then electric power and the cost of electrical energy. This is one of the most numerical chapters in Class 10 Science. Redraw each circuit neatly, find the total resistance first, and always write units.",
  "physics|10|Magnetic Effects of Electric Current":
    "A current in a wire produces a magnetic field, and this chapter explores what follows from that. It covers magnetic field lines, the field around a straight wire, a loop and a solenoid, and the right-hand thumb rule. You will learn how a field exerts a force on a conductor carrying current, using Fleming's left-hand rule. The chapter ends with domestic circuits, earthing, short circuits, overloading and fuses. Practise drawing field lines with arrows and using each hand rule, since direction questions are common.",

  // ---------- Class 10 Maths ----------
  "maths|10|Real Numbers":
    "Every composite number can be written as a product of primes in exactly one way, apart from the order. This chapter uses that fact, the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, to find HCF and LCM and to solve word problems about bells, tiles and equal groups. It then proves that numbers such as √2, √3 and √5 are irrational, using proof by contradiction. Learn to tell from the wording whether a problem needs HCF or LCM, and practise the irrationality proof step by step, because it is asked regularly.",
  "maths|10|Polynomials":
    "A zero of a polynomial is a value that makes it equal to 0, and on a graph it is where the curve meets the x-axis. This chapter connects zeroes with graphs and with coefficients. You will find the zeroes of quadratic polynomials by splitting the middle term, use their sum and product, and form a polynomial from given zeroes. Cubic polynomials are touched on as well. Remember the signs in the sum and product relations, and practise expressions such as the sum of the squares of the zeroes.",
  "maths|10|Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables":
    "Two linear equations together can have one solution, no solution or infinitely many. This chapter shows how to find out which, by graphs and by comparing the ratios of coefficients. You will solve pairs by the graphical, substitution and elimination methods, and find unknown constants such as k. Word problems on ages, digits, money, angles and fixed charges make up a large part of the practice. Begin every word problem by stating what x and y stand for, then form the equations.",
  "maths|10|Quadratic Equations":
    "A quadratic equation has the form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a not equal to zero. This chapter covers recognising quadratic equations, forming them from real situations, and solving them by factorisation and by the quadratic formula. The discriminant tells you whether the roots are real and distinct, real and equal, or not real. In word problems, check both roots and reject any that make no sense, such as a negative length. Questions on the nature of roots and finding k are very common.",
  "maths|10|Arithmetic Progressions":
    "In an arithmetic progression each term is found by adding the same number to the one before it. This chapter covers the common difference, the nth term, finding terms from the end, checking whether a number belongs to the list, and the sum of the first n terms. Real examples include rows of logs, rows of plants and savings that grow steadily. Write down a, d and n before using any formula. Decide whether the question is about a single term or a sum, which is where mistakes often begin.",
  "maths|10|Triangles":
    "Similar figures have the same shape but not always the same size. This chapter begins with similar polygons and the Basic Proportionality Theorem with its converse, and then gives the AAA, SSS and SAS criteria for similar triangles. You will use similarity to find unknown lengths, to prove results, and to work with heights and shadows. Write the vertices of similar triangles in matching order, as this keeps the ratios right. The proof of the Basic Proportionality Theorem is worth learning thoroughly.",
  "maths|10|Coordinate Geometry":
    "This chapter uses numbers to answer questions about points and shapes. The distance formula gives the length between two points and helps you decide whether a triangle is isosceles or right-angled, or whether four points form a square. The section formula finds the point that divides a line segment in a given ratio, and the mid-point formula is its simplest case. Practise finding unknown coordinates and the ratio in which an axis cuts a line. Take care with negative signs when you substitute.",
  "maths|10|Introduction to Trigonometry":
    "Trigonometry connects the angles of a right triangle with the ratios of its sides. This chapter defines sine, cosine, tangent and their reciprocals, and finds their values for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90°. It then proves the main identities, beginning with sin²A + cos²A = 1, and uses them to simplify expressions. Learn the table of values well and practise proving identities, starting from the more complicated side. If one ratio is given, draw a triangle and find the rest from it.",
  "maths|10|Some Applications of Trigonometry":
    "This chapter uses trigonometry to find heights and distances that cannot be measured directly. You will learn the line of sight, and the angles of elevation and depression, and then solve problems on towers, shadows, ladders, broken trees, rivers and two buildings. The key is a correct diagram: mark the right angle, the known lengths and the angles, then choose the ratio that links what you know with what you need. Most answers involve √3, so practise leaving them in exact form before rounding.",
  "maths|10|Circles":
    "A tangent touches a circle at exactly one point. This chapter covers the possible positions of a line and a circle, the number of tangents from a point, and two important theorems: a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, and the tangents drawn from an external point are equal in length. These lead to problems on concentric circles, angles between tangents and quadrilaterals drawn around a circle. Join the centre to the points of contact first, and look for right triangles.",
  "maths|10|Areas Related to Circles":
    "This chapter finds the area and length of parts of a circle. You will use the angle at the centre to work out the area of a sector, the length of its arc, and the area of a segment as a sector minus a triangle. Everyday problems include clock hands, windscreen wipers, umbrellas, grazing animals and table covers. Check whether a question asks for the minor or the major part, and whether it gives the radius or the diameter. Learn the triangle areas for 60°, 90° and 120°, which appear often.",
  "maths|10|Surface Areas and Volumes":
    "Many objects are made by joining simple solids, such as a cone on a hemisphere or a cylinder with hemispherical ends. This chapter finds the surface area and volume of such combinations, and deals with solids that are melted or poured into another shape. Remember that faces joined together are hidden, so they do not count in the surface area, while volumes simply add up. List the formulas you need, use the same units throughout, and leave π in your working until the last step where possible.",
  "maths|10|Statistics":
    "This chapter finds the mean, median and mode of grouped data. The mean can be worked out by the direct, assumed mean or step-deviation method, and all three are practised here. For the median you need cumulative frequency and the median class, and for the mode you need the modal class. Some questions ask for a missing frequency or for the most suitable measure. Set out a neat table with every column labelled, and learn what each symbol in the formulas stands for before you substitute.",
  "maths|10|Probability":
    "Theoretical probability is the number of favourable outcomes divided by the number of all equally likely outcomes. This chapter applies that idea to coins, dice, playing cards, spinners and balls in a bag. You will also learn about sure and impossible events and about complementary events, whose probabilities add up to 1. List outcomes carefully, especially for two dice, where there are 36. Know the make-up of a pack of cards well, including the number of face cards, since many questions depend on it.",

  // ---------- Class 10 English ----------
  "english|10|A Letter to God":
    "A hailstorm destroys the crops of Lencho, a farmer with complete faith, so he writes a letter asking God for money. What the postmaster does next, and how Lencho replies, gives the story its irony. Practice covers the setting, the storm, the two letters, the characters and the meaning of the ending, with extract-based questions. Vocabulary work includes glossary words and names for different storms. Think about the gap between what Lencho believes and what the reader knows, since that is the heart of most long answers.",
  "english|10|Nelson Mandela: Long Walk to Freedom":
    "This extract from Nelson Mandela's autobiography describes the day South Africa's first democratic government was sworn in, and his thoughts on courage, freedom and duty. Practice covers the ceremony, the sacrifices of earlier patriots, the twin obligations of a person, and how Mandela's idea of freedom grew. You will explain lines such as those on love and hate, and why the oppressor must be freed too. Vocabulary questions include words from the speech. Keep short quotations ready, as they make answers stronger.",
  "english|10|Two Stories about Flying":
    "The first story follows a young seagull who is afraid to fly until hunger and his mother's clever trick push him off the ledge. The second is about a pilot lost in storm clouds who is guided by a mysterious black aeroplane. Both are about fear, courage and help. Practice covers events, feelings, the theme of each story and the question left unanswered at the end, along with similes and vocabulary. Compare how fear is overcome in the two stories, as this makes a good long answer.",
  "english|10|From the Diary of Anne Frank":
    "Anne Frank writes to her diary, which she calls Kitty, because she feels that paper has more patience than people. This extract gives a short account of her life and an amusing story about her teacher, Mr Keesing, and the essays he makes her write. Practice covers why Anne keeps a diary, her family, the essays and how her teacher changes. Language work includes compound words, phrasal verbs, idioms and contracted forms. Notice how Anne uses humour to win an argument, and be ready to describe her character.",
  "english|10|Glimpses of India":
    "This chapter brings together three pictures of India: the baker of Goa and his place in childhood memories, the forests and people of Coorg, and a journey through the tea gardens of Assam. Practice covers customs, landscape, wildlife, history and the small details that make each place special. Vocabulary questions include words from travel and adventure, such as canoeing and rappelling. Make separate notes for each section, because questions may ask for facts from any one of them or for a comparison between them.",
  "english|10|Mijbil the Otter":
    "Gavin Maxwell tells how he came to own an otter named Mijbil and brought it home from Iraq to London. The account is full of affection and comedy, from the discovery of the bathroom tap to the trouble in the aeroplane. Practice covers Mij's habits, his games, the journey, the helpful air hostess and the bond between the two. You will also work on similes, comparisons and vocabulary. Remember the order of events, and look for lines that show how carefully the writer watches the animal.",
  "english|10|Madam Rides the Bus":
    "Valli, a curious village girl, saves her money for one great adventure: a bus ride to the town and back. The story follows her planning, her pride in being treated as a grown-up, the sights on the road and the sad scene on the way home. Practice covers Valli's character, the people on the bus, the sequence of the journey and how her mood changes. Vocabulary work includes words such as wistfully and haughtily. Think about what she learns that day about life and death.",
  "english|10|The Sermon at Benares":
    "This chapter tells how Gautama became the Buddha and recalls his first sermon at Benares. At its centre is Kisa Gotami, who asks for medicine for her dead son and is sent to find mustard seed from a house where no one has died. Practice covers the four sights, the search, the Buddha's teaching on grief and the comparisons he uses. Language questions include older phrases, glossary words and joining clauses. Be ready to explain in your own words why the wise do not grieve.",
  "english|10|The Proposal":
    "In this one-act comedy by Anton Chekhov, Lomov comes to ask for Natalya's hand but ends up quarrelling with her over Oxen Meadows and then over their dogs. The play laughs at pride, bad temper and marriages made for convenience. Practice covers the characters, the two quarrels, the humour and who said what, along with vocabulary and a good deal of reported speech. Read it aloud with friends to enjoy the comedy, and practise changing dialogue into reported speech, which the questions test often.",

  // ---------- Class 10 Social Science ----------
  "history|10|The Rise of Nationalism in Europe":
    "In the nineteenth century, people in Europe began to think of themselves as nations. This chapter starts with the French Revolution and Napoleon's reforms, then covers liberalism, conservatism after the Congress of Vienna, the revolutions of 1830 and 1848, and the role of language, art and romanticism. It explains how Germany and Italy were unified, how Britain was made, and how nations were shown as symbols. The last part links nationalism with imperialism. Learn the order of events, and what each symbol in a picture stands for.",
  "history|10|Nationalism in India":
    "This chapter follows the national movement from the First World War to the Quit India Movement. It covers satyagraha, the Rowlatt Act, Jallianwalla Bagh, Khilafat and the Non-Cooperation Movement in towns, villages and plantations. The Salt March and Civil Disobedience come next, with the views of different groups who took part and the limits of the movement. It ends with how songs, symbols and images built a feeling of belonging. Make a timeline, and for each movement note who joined, why, and why it ended.",
  "history|10|The Making of a Global World":
    "Globalisation is much older than the internet. This chapter begins with silk routes, the spread of food crops, and conquest and disease, then shows how a world economy took shape in the nineteenth century through trade, migration and capital. It covers rinderpest, indentured labour and colonial trade, followed by the inter-war economy, the Great Depression, and the rebuilding of the world economy after 1945. Focus on links between events, such as how a fall in prices abroad affected Indian farmers.",
  "history|10|The Age of Industrialisation":
    "This chapter asks how industry really grew, and shows that change was slower and more uneven than is often believed. It covers proto-industrialisation, the coming of factories, and why hand labour remained important in Britain. It then turns to India: weavers under the East India Company, the effect of Manchester cloth, early entrepreneurs, factory workers and small-scale industries, and how advertisements built a market for goods. Learn the reasons behind each change, since many questions begin with 'why'.",
  "history|10|Print Culture and the Modern World":
    "Printing changed how people think. This chapter starts with hand printing in China, Japan and Korea, moves to Gutenberg's press and the print revolution in Europe, and shows how books encouraged debate, dissent and new kinds of readers. It then looks at India: manuscripts, the arrival of the press, religious reform, women readers, cheap books for the poor, and censorship under colonial rule. Connect each change in print with its effect on society, and remember the key people, places and dates.",
  "geography|10|Resources and Development":
    "Anything in our environment that we can use, with the technology available, is a resource. This chapter classifies resources in different ways and explains why planning and sustainable development are needed. It covers land use in India and land degradation, and then the main soil types, including alluvial, black, laterite, arid and forest soils, with their features and crops. Soil erosion and conservation complete the chapter. Learn each soil with its region, colour and crops, and practise marking them on an outline map.",
  "geography|10|Forest and Wildlife Resources":
    "India has a rich variety of plants and animals, and this chapter explains why they need protection. It covers biodiversity, the importance of forests, the Wildlife (Protection) Act of 1972, Project Tiger and other special projects, and the types of forests. It also shows how communities protect nature through sacred groves and Joint Forest Management. The chapter is short and full of facts. Remember the examples of community action, because they are the usual basis of longer answers, and learn the main dates in order.",
  "geography|10|Water Resources":
    "Water covers much of the Earth, yet fresh water is scarce in many places. This chapter explains the reasons for water scarcity, including overuse, pollution and unequal access. It looks at ancient water structures, dams and multi-purpose projects with their benefits and problems, and inter-state disputes. It then describes rainwater harvesting, from rooftop tanks in Rajasthan to bamboo drip irrigation, and government schemes. Prepare both sides of the argument about dams, and learn one example of water harvesting from each region.",
  "geography|10|Agriculture":
    "Agriculture supports a large share of India's people. This chapter compares primitive subsistence, intensive subsistence, commercial and plantation farming, and explains the rabi, kharif and zaid cropping seasons. It covers the main crops, such as rice, wheat, millets, maize, pulses, sugarcane, oilseeds, tea, coffee, rubber, cotton and jute, with the conditions each one needs. Reforms and support for farmers come at the end. A table of crops with temperature, rainfall, soil and leading states is the best way to revise.",
  "geography|10|Minerals and Energy Resources":
    "Minerals are part of almost everything we use. This chapter explains what minerals are, how they occur, and where iron ore, manganese, copper, bauxite, mica and limestone are found in India. It discusses the hazards of mining and the need to conserve minerals. The second half covers energy: coal, petroleum, natural gas and electricity, along with non-conventional sources such as solar, wind, biogas and tidal energy. Map work is important here, so practise marking the iron ore belts, coalfields and other main locations.",
  "geography|10|Manufacturing Industries":
    "Manufacturing turns raw materials into more valuable goods. This chapter explains why industry matters and how it is classified by raw material, role, capital, ownership and weight. It then studies cotton and jute textiles, sugar, iron and steel, aluminium, chemicals, fertilisers, cement, automobiles and information technology. The final part is about industrial pollution and how it can be controlled. For each industry, learn the raw materials and the reasons for its location, and be ready to suggest practical ways to reduce pollution.",
  "geography|10|Lifelines of National Economy":
    "Transport, communication and trade keep a country moving, which is why they are called its lifelines. This chapter covers roadways and their classes, railways, waterways, pipelines, sea ports and airways, with the advantages and problems of each. It also explains communication, international trade and tourism. Compare the means of transport by cost, speed and the goods they suit. Map questions on ports, airports and national highways are common, so keep an outline map beside you while you revise.",
  "politics|10|Power-sharing":
    "Who should hold power in a country with many communities? This chapter compares Belgium, which found a way to accommodate its language groups, with Sri Lanka, where majoritarian policies led to conflict. From these stories it draws out two kinds of reasons for sharing power, prudential and moral. It then explains the forms power sharing can take: among organs of government, between levels, among social groups and among parties. Learn the arrangements in Belgium point by point, as they are asked very often.",
  "politics|10|Federalism":
    "Federalism divides power between a central government and its units. This chapter explains its features, and the difference between coming together and holding together federations. It shows what makes India federal: the Union, State and Concurrent Lists, the role of courts, linguistic states, language policy and Centre-State relations. The last part is about decentralisation, with Panchayati Raj, the Gram Sabha and urban local bodies. Learn examples of subjects in each list, and the main changes made to strengthen local government.",
  "politics|10|Gender, Religion and Caste":
    "Social differences can become political, and this chapter looks at three of them in India. Under gender it covers the division of labour, women's work, education and representation in legislatures. Under religion it explains communalism, its forms, and the meaning of a secular state. Under caste it discusses inequality, how caste has changed, and the two-way link between caste and politics. Use data and examples in your answers, and be clear that expressing social differences in politics can have both good and bad results.",
  "politics|10|Political Parties":
    "Modern democracy can hardly work without political parties. This chapter explains what a party is and its components, the functions it performs, and why parties are necessary. It compares one-party, two-party and multi-party systems, and separates national from state parties. It then discusses the challenges parties face, such as dynastic succession, money and muscle power and a lack of internal democracy, along with ways to reform them. Functions, challenges and reforms are the three lists to learn well.",
  "politics|10|Outcomes of Democracy":
    "Is democracy really better than other forms of government? This chapter judges it by its results. It asks whether democracy gives an accountable and legitimate government, whether it brings economic growth and reduces inequality, and how well it handles social diversity and protects the dignity and freedom of citizens. The answers are not all positive, so a balanced view is needed. For each outcome, prepare one point in favour, one limit, and an example, and you will be ready for most long questions.",
  "economics|10|Development":
    "People want different things, so development means different things to different people. This chapter compares the goals of individuals and groups, and shows why income alone is not enough to measure progress. It explains per capita income, the World Bank classification, health and education indicators, Body Mass Index and the Human Development Report. The last part is about sustainability and the use of resources. Practise reading the tables and finding averages, and remember why an average can hide inequality.",
  "economics|10|Sectors of the Indian Economy":
    "Economic activity is grouped into the primary, secondary and tertiary sectors. This chapter explains how they depend on one another, how production is counted using final goods, GDP and GVA, and why the share of services has grown. It discusses underemployment and disguised unemployment, ways to create jobs including MGNREGA, and the differences between organised and unorganised and between public and private sectors. Practise classifying jobs into sectors, and reading graphs that compare output with employment.",
  "economics|10|Money and Credit":
    "Money removes the need for a double coincidence of wants, which makes barter difficult. This chapter explains modern forms of money, bank deposits and cheques, and how banks use deposits to give loans. It then looks at credit, showing with examples how it can help a borrower or lead into a debt trap. You will compare formal and informal sources, terms of credit, cooperatives and Self Help Groups. Learn the meaning of collateral, and be ready to explain why formal credit needs to reach the poor.",
  "economics|10|Globalisation and the Indian Economy":
    "Goods, services, investment and technology now move across countries more easily than ever. This chapter explains how multinational corporations spread production, the meaning of foreign trade and foreign investment, and how markets become connected. It covers the factors behind globalisation, including technology, liberalisation and the World Trade Organisation, and its effects on consumers, workers and small producers in India. It ends with the idea of fair globalisation. Prepare gains and losses for each group, with an example for both.",
  "economics|10|Consumer Rights":
    "Consumers can be harmed by unfair trade practices, false information or unsafe goods. This chapter explains why markets can be unfair, how the consumer movement grew, and the rights a consumer has: to safety, to be informed, to choose, to seek redressal, to representation and to consumer education. Case stories show how people took complaints to Consumer Commissions. It also covers quality marks and the duties of an alert consumer. Match each case with the right it shows, since many questions are set that way.",
};
